#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505873} :activity/id #uuid "0eb4fea8-7b7f-55ef-a41d-d9f4ac3195c9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050539 :topic/id #uuid "52089b25-b5c5-5717-906a-bd765cce0502" :topic/math-academy-id 3119} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505873 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357709 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f1ffe36-0882-5870-a270-de42a9825f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355074} {:answer/id #uuid "30058854-8d3f-57d9-be58-54e0a4a41e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355082} {:answer/id #uuid "47e9181e-3767-5221-bf98-12ae5e9c516c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355303} {:answer/id #uuid "612b21e7-44a4-5ea2-a6d2-221f3d98d3d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355528} {:answer/id #uuid "9d6f4c77-e2f7-5a7d-812b-8c47c5918905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356069}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356069} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5346222-44b8-5c0f-9c03-abcf827a4a5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "22e141ba-7caa-5978-a6dc-ef43c0b1ee42" :question/math-academy-id "q-94259" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7093551b-07fc-5ff1-a4c8-030bdccede09" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505888}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505878 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "559d51e9-82ba-5eda-8611-4b261056e7e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404695} {:answer/id #uuid "f90ddf0e-0beb-53d2-ba74-1fb77d8d7148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404696} {:answer/id #uuid "b241767b-b316-5989-af0a-17abbe739dd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ \nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404697} {:answer/id #uuid "b683bcd1-ff2a-5485-8106-4a44f4178290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ \nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404698} {:answer/id #uuid "8b9ae9de-aeed-5441-801e-28fc4a080e9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404699}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404697} :answer-field/id #uuid "14dd9a0b-f864-51a4-baa2-9464da8de876" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f0919d70-c1a2-5d35-a661-b7830e88967b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94347" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-6.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}.$ \n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are  orthogonal. So, we divide ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ by their respective norms to obtain the first and second columns of $P:$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7f92a652-43f6-587a-a8b8-f62ae03dfd9c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505899}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505879 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5af1ce91-3b7a-534b-98ad-56dc4a10b93d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{22}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{22}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{22}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505287} {:answer/id #uuid "5e64e4d0-0be7-5588-9a76-c5376f91f8f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505296} {:answer/id #uuid "6f89375f-1402-57bf-9d90-6e83a80103da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505332} {:answer/id #uuid "71d799ca-d5f1-5bf0-a256-91e6f1e8a407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{22}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{22}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{22}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505337} {:answer/id #uuid "a51a893a-d1e6-5946-9669-2b709310dc79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505434}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505287} :answer-field/id #uuid "82103b11-eb72-51ff-8fe5-77b1959844e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "15341cc2-96e2-5aa8-b822-d1195f4412c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94302" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=8.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=8.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=8.$ Computing $A-8I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-8I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-8I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{22}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{22}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{22}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{22}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{22}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "95bdc2bb-7bd0-5a10-b18b-ed936f4da1af" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505878}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505888 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505766 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3941599a-696b-53ea-830d-d1c913a4233f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505203} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0876c1-b9fa-512d-a8e4-15d9833aa8b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505323} {:answer/id #uuid "75201209-b00b-5547-ad21-233cf3e895fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505342} {:answer/id #uuid "b84762ba-f3d5-5c77-a3c0-f89a04da9c39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505462} {:answer/id #uuid "f8eab7a0-e6d3-5ac8-b5dc-c82824dcfe21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505589}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505589} :answer-field/id #uuid "86f33f22-e55a-5156-aed2-3b2e2360e08f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "548a890f-5ff5-5b4e-b39d-7fd5f8a56600" :question/math-academy-id "q-94264" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c90bbda8-2d90-5c6d-95b2-4a5ad309bb13" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505879}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d479be-872e-5b2b-9a6f-55bc9a392083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{30} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{15}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363198} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef4f44e-6d79-526b-a93e-f229a1ed3dfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{-2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363199} {:answer/id #uuid "be75fdd8-49c3-5720-aa14-5bf6b23f6f2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363200} {:answer/id #uuid "f288e8dc-d03c-5849-9de6-7f832f534bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363201} {:answer/id #uuid "312d113a-1309-533b-bf4f-3d3a3da1e08c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363202}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363198} :answer-field/id #uuid "43da5e18-1a97-5649-82f0-f3809dd899d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bf5de7bd-3234-5d41-a9a5-c12fb9a085ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-140841" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=12.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}.$ \n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that correspond to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=6.$ Computing $A-6I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n\n$$\n{e}_{1}={v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we find ${e}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}+\\frac{2}{5}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{2}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}_{1}}{‖{e}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{e}_{2}}{‖{e}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{5})}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{30} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{15}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "edb9db9d-0aa0-5ee6-86cf-b0c3b99d1764"}] :activity/title "Diagonalization of 3x3 Symmetric Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505094}]}
